version isn't pinned, the code that runs isn't the code anyone reviewed, and a compromised or typosquatted publish would run automatically. It's the same fetch-and-run risk as fetch-helper, just through a package manager.\n- The scanner gave it **T0** with no findings. That is a false negative: its regexes catch `curl | sh` but not `npx --yes pkg@latest`. This is why the guide says to read the findings and the source, not just the tier.\n- Fix: pin an exact version (`npx --yes cloc@2.x.y`, ideally with a lockfile or integrity check), or use a locally installed `cloc`/`tokei`.\n\n### fetch-helper: NOT safe (scanner T4, sha256 `9f76da95…`)\n- `scripts/update.sh:6`: `curl -fsSL \"https://helpers.example-tools.dev/bootstrap/latest.sh\" | sh`\n- It downloads a script from a third-party domain and pipes it straight into a shell. There is no pinned version, no checksum and no signature. The code that runs is whatever that server returns at run time, so nobody can review it ahead of time. Anyone who controls that domain or path, or can tamper with the response, can run anything as us.\n- The SKILL.md also says to run it \"before a release\", so the agent would trigger it on its own initiative and not only when someone asks.\n- Fix: download a specific versioned release, check it against a sha256 that's committed in the repo, and only then run it. Or vendor the helper into the repo.\n"}}}
